::ALG1_U02_L07_Q01::Solve x + 14 = 39. {
=A. x = 25 #Correct. Subtract 14 from both sides.
~B. x = 53 #This adds 14 instead of subtracting.
~C. x = 14 #This is the known addend.
~D. x = 39 #This is the total, not the unknown.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q02::Solve 7p = 63. {
~A. p = 56 #This subtracts 7 instead of dividing.
=B. p = 9 #Correct. Divide both sides by 7.
~C. p = 70 #This adds 7 instead of dividing.
~D. p = 441 #This multiplies instead of dividing.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q03::Solve 3n - 8 = 34. {
~A. n = 12 #This gives 28, not 34.
~B. n = 26 #This stops after subtracting incorrectly.
=C. n = 14 #Correct. Add 8, then divide by 3.
~D. n = 42 #This stops before dividing by 3.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q04::Solve 5(x + 2) = 45. {
~A. x = 5 #This gives 35, not 45.
~B. x = 9 #This forgets to subtract 2 after dividing.
~C. x = 43 #This subtracts 2 from 45 only.
=D. x = 7 #Correct. Divide by 5, then subtract 2.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q05::Solve 6x - 11 = 2x + 17. {
=A. x = 7 #Correct. 4x = 28, so x = 7.
~B. x = 4 #This gives unequal sides.
~C. x = 28 #This stops before dividing by 4.
~D. x = -7 #The sign is incorrect.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q06::Solve x/5 + 6 = 14. {
~A. x = 8 #This stops after subtracting 6.
=B. x = 40 #Correct. Subtract 6, then multiply by 5.
~C. x = 20 #This does not satisfy the equation.
~D. x = 70 #This multiplies before subtracting 6.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q07::Solve 3(2x - 1) = 33. {
~A. x = 5 #This gives 27, not 33.
~B. x = 11 #This forgets the factor of 2 after dividing.
=C. x = 6 #Correct. Divide by 3, add 1, then divide by 2.
~D. x = 18 #This stops before isolating x.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q08::Solve 9x - 4 = 5x + 20. {
~A. x = 4 #This gives 32 and 40.
~B. x = 24 #This stops before dividing by 4.
~C. x = -6 #The sign is incorrect.
=D. x = 6 #Correct. 4x = 24, so x = 6.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q09::A phone plan costs $18 plus $4 per gigabyte. The total is $62. How many gigabytes g were used? {
=A. g = 11 #Correct. 18 + 4g = 62, so g = 11.
~B. g = 15.5 #This divides the total by 4 and ignores the base cost.
~C. g = 44 #This is the variable cost, not the number of gigabytes.
~D. g = 80 #This adds the base cost to the total.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q10::Solve P = 2l + 2w for l. {
~A. l = P - 2w #This does not divide by 2.
=B. l = (P - 2w)/2 #Correct. Subtract 2w, then divide by 2.
~C. l = (P + 2w)/2 #The 2w term should be subtracted.
~D. l = P/2 - 2w #Only after subtracting 2w should the expression be divided by 2.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q11::Which first step is best for 4(x - 3) = 28? {
~A. Add 3 to both sides. #The 3 is inside parentheses.
~B. Subtract 4 from both sides. #The 4 is multiplying the group.
=C. Divide both sides by 4. #Correct. This removes the outside factor efficiently.
~D. Divide both sides by 3. #The expression is not divided by 3.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q12::A field trip costs $75 plus $9 per student. The total is $318. How many students s are going? {
~A. s = 35.3 #This divides the total by 9 and ignores the fixed cost.
~B. s = 243 #This is the student-cost amount, not the number of students.
~C. s = 36 #This adds before dividing and does not match the context.
=D. s = 27 #Correct. 75 + 9s = 318, so s = 27.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q13::Solve y = mx + b for m. {
=A. m = (y - b)/x #Correct. Subtract b, then divide by x.
~B. m = y - b - x #Subtraction does not undo multiplication by x.
~C. m = x/(y - b) #The quotient is reversed.
~D. m = (y + b)/x #The b term should be subtracted.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q14::Solve 8x + 6 = 3x + 41. {
~A. x = 35 #This stops before dividing by 5.
=B. x = 7 #Correct. 5x = 35, so x = 7.
~C. x = 5 #This does not satisfy the equation.
~D. x = -7 #The sign is incorrect.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q15::Which equation type is 7x - 5 = 4x + 16? {
~A. One-step equation #More than one operation and variables are on both sides.
~B. Literal equation #This is not a formula being solved for a specified variable.
=C. Equation with variables on both sides #Correct. Variable terms appear on both sides.
~D. No-solution equation #It has a solution, but the type is variables on both sides.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q16::A student solves 2x + 11 = 35 and writes x = 24. What did the student forget? {
~A. To add 11 #The 11 should be subtracted.
~B. To subtract 2 #The coefficient 2 requires division.
~C. To move variables to both sides #There is only one variable term.
=D. To divide by 2 after subtracting 11 #Correct. 2x = 24, so x = 12.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q17::Solve 2x + 11 = 35. {
=A. x = 12 #Correct. Subtract 11, then divide by 2.
~B. x = 24 #This stops before dividing by 2.
~C. x = 46 #This adds instead of subtracting 11.
~D. x = 17.5 #This divides before subtracting 11.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q18::Solve d = rt for t. {
~A. t = d - r #Subtraction does not undo multiplication by r.
=B. t = d/r #Correct. Divide both sides by r.
~C. t = r/d #The quotient is reversed.
~D. t = dr #Multiplication does not isolate t.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q19::A subscription costs $12 plus $7 per month. The total is $82. How many months m are included? {
~A. m = 11.7 #This divides the total by 7 and ignores the fixed cost.
~B. m = 70 #This is the monthly-cost total, not the number of months.
=C. m = 10 #Correct. 12 + 7m = 82, so m = 10.
~D. m = 94 #This adds instead of solving.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q20::Which statement is true for 3x + 6 = 3(x + 2)? {
~A. It has no solution. #Both sides simplify to the same expression.
~B. It has the solution x = 0 only. #Every value of x works.
~C. It has the solution x = 2 only. #Every value of x works.
=D. It has infinitely many solutions. #Correct. Both sides are equivalent.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q21::Which strategy best fits a context problem with a starting fee plus a constant rate? {
=A. Define a variable, write fixed fee plus rate times variable equals total, then solve. #Correct. This matches the structure of many two-step applications.
~B. Divide the total by the starting fee only. #This ignores the rate.
~C. Always move variables to both sides first. #The variable may appear on only one side.
~D. Rearrange a formula before reading the context. #The context determines the equation.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q22::Solve 10 - 2x = 28. {
~A. x = 9 #The sign is incorrect.
=B. x = -9 #Correct. -2x = 18, so x = -9.
~C. x = 19 #This adds without accounting for -2.
~D. x = -19 #This does not satisfy the equation.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q23::Solve 4x + 8 = 4x - 3. {
~A. x = 11 #The variable terms cancel, leaving a false statement.
~B. x = -11 #The variable terms cancel, leaving a false statement.
=C. No solution #Correct. 8 = -3 is false.
~D. Infinitely many solutions #The constants are not equal after variables cancel.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q24::Solve 5(x + 1) - 2 = 28. {
~A. x = 4 #This gives 23, not 28.
~B. x = 6 #This forgets to subtract 1 after solving the group.
~C. x = 31/5 #This combines constants incorrectly.
=D. x = 5 #Correct. 5x + 3 = 28, so x = 5.
}

::ALG1_U02_L07_Q25::Why is method selection important in mixed equation applications? {
=A. Different equation structures require different first steps. #Correct. Structure guides the solving strategy.
~B. It changes the meaning of the original problem. #The goal is to preserve meaning.
~C. It lets you ignore checking. #Checking remains important.
~D. It removes the need to define variables. #Variables should still be defined in contexts.
}
